<!-- the Riemann sum calculator used in this Preview is due to Peter Keep -->
<!-- see Peter's original work at https://beta.doenet.org/activityViewer/anvfo1993TKgun9i4sWeLM -->
<!-- a rough draft of this ACS Preview was completed by Jacob Levin after the 2024 Doenet workshop -->

<setup>
  <!-- Calculate the offset: sample point x_i^* = x_i + offset -->
  <!-- Left, Mid and Right Endpoint Rule corresponds to 0, 1, 2 in following computation -->
  <number name="ruleChoiceNumber">
  <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Left Riemann Sum">0</case>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Midpoint Riemann Sum">1</case>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Right Riemann Sum">2</case>
  </conditionalContent>
  </number>
  <number name="offset">$dx*$ruleChoiceNumber / 2</number>
  <!-- numeric version of dx in case symbolic version (displayed to user) slows things down -->
  <math simplify="true" name="dx">($b-$a)/$n</math>
  <number name="dxN">$dx</number>


  <!-- Compute the x_i's here, instead of within the graph -->
  <sequence from="$a" to="$b-$dx" step="$dx" name="xiValues" />
  <!-- For help in zooming into the graph correctly: what are the minimum and maximum rectangle heights? -->
  <!-- make sure to to include 0 in the computations, in case all heights are positive (or negative) -->
  <!-- note: could use $$f($xVal+$offset), but tacking $$f($b) on the computation means less shifting
      of the graph when switching between rules (if rectanges stretch beyond the graph with right 
      endpoints but not left, for example.  Less shifting seems better -->
  <setup><group name="group">$xiValues</group></setup><repeat for="$group" name="fValues" valueName="xVal">$$f($xVal)</repeat>
  <number name="minY"><min>0 $fValues $$f($b)</min></number>
  <number name="maxY"><max>0 $fValues $$f($b)</max></number>

  <mathList name="accOrder"> M_3 T_3 L_3 R_3</mathList>
  
</setup>

<p>
  As in Preview Activity 4.3.1, the graph provided below includes a Riemann sum calculator: in the boxed windows, you can input your own function <m>f(x)</m>, your own interval from <m>x=a</m> to <m>x=b</m>, and the number of rectangles, <m>n</m>, you'd like to use.  Then, from the drop-down menu choose your strategy for selecting the <m>x</m>-values from each of the subintervals (a Left, Midpoint, or Right Riemann sum).  The resulting value of the Riemann sum will be computed and displayed below the graph. 
</p>
<p>
  <alert>Note:</alert> you have to hit the <q>enter</q> key after anything you change in one of these boxes in order to update the resulting computations.  Computations involving larger <m>n</m> values can take a few seconds to appear.
</p>

<!-- Start by definining the function, a, b, and n (and hence dx), and rule choice -->
<tabular>
    <row>
      <cell halign="right"><m>f(x) = </m><mathInput prefill="x^2" name="fnFormula" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>a =</m><mathInput name="a" prefill="0" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>b =</m><mathInput name="b" prefill="3" /></cell>
      <cell halign="right"><m>n =</m><mathInput prefill="3" name="n" /></cell>
      <!--<cell halign="right"><m>\Delta x = <math simplify="true" name="dx">($b-$a)/$n</math></m></cell>-->
    </row>
</tabular>
<tabular>
    <row>
      <cell halign="left">choice for <m>x_k^*</m>:<choiceInput name="ruleChoice" inline="true" preselectChoice="1">
          <choice>Left Riemann Sum</choice>
          <choice>Midpoint Riemann Sum</choice>
          <choice>Right Riemann Sum</choice>
      </choiceInput>
      </cell>
    </row>
</tabular>

<!-- Graph the function, points at (a,0) and (b,0), and then the n rectangles -->
<graph size="medium" xmin="$a-1.5" xmax="$b+1.5" ymin="$minY-1" ymax="$maxY+1" identicalAxisScales="false" showNavigation="false" fixAxes>
  <function name="f" layer="0" symbolic="true">$fnFormula</function>
  <regionBetweenCurveXAxis function="$f" boundaryValues="$a $b" />

<repeatForSequence from="$a" to="$b-$dxN" step="$dxN" valueName="xVal">
    <polygon stylenumber="6" filled="true" draggable="false" vertices="($xVal,0) 
                ($xVal+$dxN,0) 
                ($xVal+$dxN, $$f($xVal+$offset)) 
                ($xVal,$$f($xVal+$offset))" />
  </repeatForSequence>  
</graph>



<p>
  The value of the Riemann sum is: <conditionalContent>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Left Riemann Sum">
      <m>L_{$n}=</m>
    </case>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Right Riemann Sum">
      <m>R_{$n}=</m>
    </case>
    <case condition="$ruleChoice=Midpoint Riemann Sum">
      <m>M_{$n}=</m>
    </case>
  </conditionalContent>
  <sum displaydecimals="6">
    <setup><group name="group1">$xiValues</group></setup><repeat for="$group1" valueName="xVal"><number>$$f($xVal+$offset)*$dx</number></repeat>
  </sum>.
</p>

<p>
  As we begin to investigate ways to approximate definite integrals, it will be insightful to compare results to integrals whose exact values we know. To that end, the following sequence of questions centers on <math>int_0^3 x^2 dx</math>.
</p>


<!-- Interactive Answers -->
<p>
  
<ol level = "2">
  <li>
    <p>
      Use the Riemann sum calculator with the function <math>f(x) = x^2</math> on the interval <m>[0,3]</m> to compute <math>L_3</math>, the left Riemann sum with three subinvtervals.
    </p>
  </li>

  <p>
    <m>L_3 = </m> <answer> 5 </answer>
  </p>

  <li>
    <p>
      Likewise, compute <math>R_3</math> and <math>M_3</math>,
 the right and middle Riemann sums with three subintervals, respectively.
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>R_3 = </m> <answer> 14 </answer>
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>M_3 = </m> <answer> 8.75 </answer>
    </p>
  </li>



  <li>
    <p>
      Use the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus to compute the exact value of <m>I = \int_0^3 x^2 dx</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>I = \int_0^3 x^2 dx = </m> <answer> 9 </answer>
    </p>
  </li>



  <li>
    <p>
      We define the <em>error</em> that results from an approximation of a definite integral to be the approximation's value minus the integral's exact value. What is the error that results from using <math>L_3</math>? From <math>R_3</math>? From <math>M_3</math>?
    </p>
    <p>
       The error for <m>L_3</m> is <answer> 5-9 </answer>
    </p>
    <p>
       The error for <m>R_3</m> is <answer> 14-9 </answer>
    </p>
    <p>
       The error for <m>M_3</m> is <answer> 8.75-9 </answer>
    </p>
  </li>
  
  <li>
    <p>
      In what follows in this section, we will learn a new apporach to estimating the value of a definite integral known as the Trapezoid Rule. The basic idea is to use trapezoids, rather than rectangles, to estimate the area under a curve. What is the formula for the area of a trapezoid with bases of length <m>b_1</m> and <m>b_2</m> and height <m>h</m>?
    </p>
  </li>

  <p>
    The area, <m>A</m>, of a trapezoid with bases of length <m>b_1</m> and <m>b_2</m> and height <m>h</m> is
    <answer>
      <choiceInput>
        <choice credit = "0"><m>A = (b_1+b_2) h</m></choice>
        <choice credit = "1"><m>A = \left(\frac{b_1+b_2}{2} \right) h</m></choice>
        <choice credit = "0"><m>A = \frac{b_1 \cdot b_2 \cdot h}{2}</m></choice>
        <choice credit = "0"><m>A = b_1 \cdot h</m></choice>
      </choiceInput>
    </answer>
  </p>

  <li>
    <p>
      Working by hand, estimate the area under <math>f(x) = x^2</math> on <math>[0,3]</math> using three subintervals and three corresponding trapezoids. Draw a careful graph and sketch in the trapezoids you use.  Call the resulting estimate <m>T_3</m>, and report both the value of <m>T_3</m> and its corresponding error below.
    </p>
    <p>
      <m>T_3 = </m> <answer> 9.5 </answer>
    </p>
    <p>
       The error for <m>T_3</m> is <answer> 9.5-9 </answer>
    </p>
  </li>
  <li>
    <p>
      Order the four approximations (<m>L_3</m>, <m>R_3</m>, <m>M_3</m>,  <m>T_3</m>) from best to worst (that is, from least absolute error to most absolute error), separating your entries with commas.  Remember to enter <q>T_3</q> in order to get the subscript.
    </p>
    <p>
      <mathInput name="ranking" minWidth="100"/><answer><award><when>$ranking=$accOrder</when></award></answer>
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>
</p>